      title: 'Python setup: virtual environments and packages',
      minutes: 6,
      body: [
        "**The problem.** Python code relies on [[package|packages]]: code other people wrote, such as `httpx` for calling APIs. Project A needs one version, project B needs another. Install everything into one shared Python and they clash. On a Mac with Homebrew, and on recent Ubuntu, `pip install` outside a project may refuse outright with `externally-managed-environment`.",
        "A [[virtual environment]] is a private Python for one project, with its own packages. It lives in a folder, usually `.venv`, and is never shared or committed. Think of each project getting its own toolbox instead of everyone grabbing from one messy drawer.",
        "1. `python3 -m venv .venv` creates it (once per project).\n2. `source .venv/bin/activate` switches it on (same on Mac and WSL). `source` runs a file’s commands in your current terminal. Your prompt now starts with `(.venv)`.\n3. `pip install httpx` installs into this project only. [[pip]] is Python’s package installer.\n4. `pip freeze > requirements.txt` records the exact versions. `>` sends a command’s output into a file, replacing what’s there; `>>` adds to the end instead. Anyone can recreate them with `pip install -r requirements.txt`.",
        "Activation lasts for that terminal window only. Open a new one and you must activate again. The classic symptom of forgetting: `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'httpx'`, even though you installed it. `which python` prints which Python would run; if the path isn’t inside `.venv`, it isn’t switched on.",
        "**Pinning.** `httpx==0.27.2` means exactly that version, so what worked today installs the same next month, and on the server.",
        "Two more tools you’ll use all course: [[pytest]] runs your tests, and **Ruff** tidies your code’s layout and flags likely mistakes (`ruff format .` and `ruff check .`).",
      ],
      example: {
        caption: 'Setting up a project’s Python, once',
        code: '$ cd ~/projects/automation-portfolio\n# create the private Python\n$ python3 -m venv .venv\n# switch it on\n$ source .venv/bin/activate\n(.venv) $ pip install httpx pytest ruff\n# record exact versions\n(.venv) $ pip freeze > requirements.txt\n# which Python is running?\n(.venv) $ which python\n/Users/ana/projects/automation-portfolio/.venv/bin/python\n# add .venv/ to .gitignore: never commit it\n(.venv) $ echo ".venv/" >> .gitignore',
      },
      interview: "Every project gets its own virtual environment, so dependencies are isolated and reproducible. I pin versions, ideally with a lock file, so CI and production install exactly what I tested. When an import fails, my first check is which interpreter is actually running.",
