      minutes: 5,
      body: [
        "**The problem.** Your script needs a HubSpot API key. The quick way is to paste it into the code. Then you push to GitHub, and anyone who reads the repo can use your key. Automated bots scan public repositories for exactly this.",
        "The fix: keep [[secret|secrets]] and settings **outside the code**. An [[environment variable]] is a named value the computer hands your program when it starts, like `HUBSPOT_TOKEN=pat-abc123`. The code only knows the *name*; the value lives elsewhere.",
        "On your laptop, that elsewhere is a [[.env]] file in the project folder: one `NAME=value` per line. The `python-dotenv` package loads it for you. On a server or in [[ci/cd|CI]] (automatic testing), the platform supplies the same names from its own secret store. Same code, different values: that’s how dev and production use different databases.",
        "The rules:",
        "- Add `.env` to `.gitignore` before your first commit.\n- Commit a `.env.example` with the same names and fake values, so others know what to set.\n- Never print or log a secret. Log *that* a key loaded, not the key.\n- If a key ever reaches GitHub, **revoke it and create a new one** at the provider. Deleting the file isn’t enough: old commits still contain it.",
        "Don’t worry about reading every line of the Python below; Python is taught in the Python module. The point here is: the real key lives only in `.env`, and the code asks for it by name. If the name is missing, `os.environ[...]` stops with a `KeyError` (Python’s “that name isn’t there” error). That’s good: better to stop at startup than run half-configured.",
      ],
      example: {
        caption: 'Keeping secrets out of the code',
        code: '# .env  (in .gitignore, never committed)\nHUBSPOT_TOKEN=pat-na1-real-secret\nDATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:devpass@localhost:5432/postgres\n\n# .env.example  (committed: same names, fake values)\nHUBSPOT_TOKEN=replace-me\nDATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/dbname\n\n# main.py  (needs: pip install python-dotenv)\nimport os\nfrom dotenv import load_dotenv\n\n# read .env into the environment\nload_dotenv()\n# KeyError if missing: fail fast\ntoken = os.environ["HUBSPOT_TOKEN"]\n# never print(token)\nprint("HubSpot token loaded")',
      },
      interview: "Configuration and secrets come from environment variables, so the same code runs in every environment. Locally they live in a gitignored .env with a committed .env.example; in production they come from the platform’s secret store. If a credential leaks, I rotate it immediately, because Git history keeps it.",
